{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310798,"nonce":"qXRcpPUunSzWlFAwGSjDa7IrxNc=","title":"MEASURED: LLVM/JIT compile peaks at 287-351 MB, NOT the 3.8 GB I claimed. OSR + lazy LLVM compile will NOT fix the 7B memory problem. The ...","message":"That figure came from a single `/tmp/boxleak` peak-RSS sample I had already\nflagged as unreliable, and I then used it to justify an OSR + lazy-compile\nproject. Measured properly (`/tmp/compilepeak.sh` — sample RSS from process\nstart until the log prints \"reading GGUF\", which separates compile from model\nload):\n\n| path | compile-phase peak | whole-run peak |\n|---|---|---|\n| prebuilt `.rzb` (the path the models use) | **351 MB** (4.1s) | 1203 MB |\n| source + `--no-cache` | **287 MB** (8.7s) | 1251 MB |\n\n**Compilation costs ~300 MB. It is not why a 4.07 GB model fails to stay\nresident on a 16 GB box.** OSR + lazy LLVM compilation would not fix the 7B\nrobustness defect. (They may still be worth doing for startup latency and for\nthe monolithic-main promotion gap that rayzor.toml documents — but NOT on a\nmemory argument.)\n\nClaimed \"~40% of compiled functions are redundant, 1638 -\u003e 962\" from the MIR\ndump. That dump is PRE-CODEGEN. Measured AT CODEGEN (post all MIR passes) on\nthe real llama-chat run:\n\n    cranelift  632 bodies, 573 distinct -\u003e 59 redundant (9%)\n    llvm       386 bodies, 327 distinct -\u003e 59 redundant (15%)\n\nand all 59 are `__vtable_init__` (48x) and `__init__` (13x) — per-file\ninitializers that MUST NOT be merged (each registers different types; the\ncollapse was a real bug, 62f69aa7). So FQN-based symbol naming buys no memory.\nIt is still correct as the ENABLER for per-function compilation — `module_counter`\nmakes symbols compile-order-dependent, which is why a cross-module Import cannot\nbe resolved at finalize (documented in beadie_jit.rs) — but sell it on that, not\non memory.\n\n**Always measure at codegen, not from the MIR dump.** Extern reuse and the\nforward-reference linking path already collapse most apparent MIR duplication.\n\nFrom `vmmap -summary` on a Mistral-7B run: the model is **file-backed, 4.1G\nvirtual / 2.8G resident / 0K swapped** and behaves correctly. The damage is\n**MALLOC_SMALL 716M virtual / 6.5M resident / 524.9M SWAPPED** and\n**DefaultMallocZone 23,768,490 allocations / 554.6M swapped**; writable regions\n49% swapped out. Anonymous pages cannot be dropped and re-read like file pages,\nso they round-trip through swap — which is why rayzor stalls (147% CPU, RSS\ncapped 3.29/4.07G) where **llama.cpp completes** (degraded but finishes).\n\n**NEXT: attribute the 23.7M allocations to a call site** (MallocStackLogging on\na short run). Do NOT start OSR for memory reasons.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_llvm_compile_is_not_the_memory_cost` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}